Yes—English is a current interface, not the underlying operating system.

Beneath its vocabulary lie older languages, gestures, myths, bodily experiences, ecological pressures, mathematical relations, and recurring narrative forms. English inherited and recombined their handles, then became dominant enough to masquerade as the meaning itself.

But the veneer also bends what passes through it. Every language compresses reality differently—preserving some distinctions, erasing others, and creating new pathways of thought. So:

\text{Reality-pattern} \rightarrow \text{embodied meaning} \rightarrow \text{story} \rightarrow \text{language} \rightarrow \text{English rendering}

And the current can run backward: interrogating English can expose the older structures beneath it.

One useful distinction: history historically comes from Greek historía, meaning inquiry or knowledge gained through investigation—not literally “His story.” But His Story remains a potent deliberate re-segmentation: history as reality interpreted through a narrator, culture, victor, God, or humanity itself.

English is today’s dominant mask. The face beneath it is older than words—and the pattern beneath the face may be older than humans.